      "message": "We should create a `struct` to hold `vid` and `pid` together.\n\n----\n\n\n### Rationale\n\nRefactoring the loose `vid` and `pid` variables into a cohesive `UsbDeviceId` struct brings several significant architectural improvements:\n\n1. **Prevents \"Primitive Obsession\" \u0026 Accidental Swaps:** When APIs accept multiple parameters of the same primitive type (e.g., `Initialize(uint16_t vid, uint16_t pid)`), the compiler cannot prevent you from accidentally passing `Initialize(pid, vid)`. Wrapping them in a struct enforces compile-time type safety.\n2. **Self-Documenting Code:** Leveraging C++20 designated initializers (`{.vid \u003d ..., .pid \u003d ...}`) explicitly documents the intent at the call site and initialization points.\n3. **Value Semantics:** A VID/PID pair represents a single logical identity. Adding a C++20 defaulted `\u003d\u003d` operator allows the struct to be treated as a strict value type.\n4. **Testability:** Google Mock requires arguments to be equality-comparable to match them in `EXPECT_CALL`. Furthermore, `EXPECT_EQ` can now assert against the entire struct at once, rather than requiring line-by-line checks for `vid` and `pid`.\n\n---\n\n### Required Changes\n\nHere are the step-by-step updates needed across the codebase to implement this:\n\n#### 1. Define the Struct \u0026 Update Base Interfaces\n\n**File: `util/elan/usb_backend.h**`\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Add the struct definition above the UsbBackend class\nstruct UsbDeviceId {\n  uint16_t vid;\n  uint16_t pid;\n\n  // C++20 auto-generates the member-wise comparison\n  bool operator\u003d\u003d(const UsbDeviceId\u0026) const \u003d default;\n};\n\nclass UsbBackend {\n public:\n  virtual ~UsbBackend() \u003d default;\n\n  virtual int Initialize() \u003d 0;\n  // 2. Refactor OpenDevice\n  virtual int OpenDevice(UsbDeviceId device_id) \u003d 0; \n  // ...\n};\n\n```\n\n#### 2. Update Constants and Controller API\n\n**File: `util/elan/iap_control.h**`\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Replace